ccdaddy is an original Go program. It vendors no third-party source and copies
none: every package in internal/ was written for this repository. Its own
license is in LICENSE.

Two things nevertheless travel with it, and this file is where they are
recorded: the notices the Go modules it links require, and what it is that
ccdaddy interoperates with.


================================================================================
Anthropic Claude Code
================================================================================

ccdaddy is an unofficial, third-party tool. It is not affiliated with,
endorsed by, sponsored by or supported by Anthropic.

It works by reading and writing the files Claude Code itself reads and writes,
and by calling the same public OAuth and usage endpoints with Claude Code's own
public client id — the ordinary way a tool interoperates with the program it
sits beside. The file layouts, the lock protocol and the endpoint constants were
established by observing the shipped Claude Code binary. No Anthropic source
code is included here, and none of it is redistributed.

"Claude" and "Claude Code" are trademarks of Anthropic, PBC. They are used here
only nominatively — to say which program this one works with — and not as a
brand for ccdaddy.


================================================================================
Go modules
================================================================================

The released binaries statically link eight third-party Go modules under
BSD-3-Clause, MIT and Apache-2.0. BSD-3-Clause and Apache-2.0 both require the
notice to accompany a BINARY distribution, so their texts are reproduced
verbatim in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt rather than linked.

That file is generated from what the binaries actually link — the union across
all six release targets, since cobra pulls in mousetrap only on Windows — by
`scripts/third-party-licenses.sh`, and a test fails when it is stale.
